 "blocks": [
  {
   "t": "notice",
   "text": "Draft for Jared's approval. Collected word for word from the city-wide rules paragraphs of the neighbourhood guides listed at the bottom (D-202); nothing new was added. Each guide keeps what is specific to it and links here.",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "t": "p",
   "text": "The rules below apply across Abbotsford, not just one neighbourhood: zoning and small-scale housing, strata rules, servicing, land rules and taxes, grouped by topic. Each neighbourhood guide keeps what is specific to that area and links back here. Rules change, so confirm anything that matters for a purchase with the City and your lawyer before you remove subjects.",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Property taxes, transfer tax and GST in Abbotsford",
   "id": "taxes",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Taxes — TWO regimes on one mountain: City part — Abbotsford taxes due July 2, 2026, 5% penalty July 3, 2026, second 5% September 16, 2026. FVRD/rural part — the owner receives the notice from the Province's Surveyor of Taxes and pays the Province, not the FVRD; under the Taxation (Rural Area) Act s.11, rural taxes are due July 2, with 5% added to any portion unpaid on July 2 and a further 5% on any portion unpaid October 31. Do not quote City dates for an FVRD parcel."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "taxes"
  },
  {
   "t": "list",
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "55+ bylaws (ss.123.1/123.2): The only age bylaw a strata may still have is 55+; exemptions expanded May 1, 2023 cover younger spouses/partners, caregivers, and children in defined cases."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "taxes"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Floodplain, farmland and environmental rules in Abbotsford",
   "id": "flood",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "t": "list",
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Zoning: Abbotsford Zoning Bylaw No. 2400-2014, rural sections: A1 Agricultural Zone (s.210): Agricultural Use and Single Detached Dwelling are principal uses; parcels created through subdivision must be a minimum of 8.0 ha; one Accessory Farm Dwelling Unit is permitted per lot only in accordance with the ALR Use Regulation, B.C. Reg. 30/2019, or as approved by the Agricultural Land Commission, on a lot of at least 0.4 ha, with a maximum floor area of 90 m² on lots of 40 ha or less and 186 m² on lots over 40 ha; one secondary suite per lot is permitted, to a maximum floor area of 100 m². RR Rural Residential Zone (s.310): minimum lot size 4.0 ha; a coach house is permitted only on a lot greater than 0.4 ha, and a coach house or secondary suite is limited to one per lot and a maximum floor area of 90 m² or 40% of the net floor area of the single detached dwelling, whichever is less. None of these added dwellings creates a separate title for sale."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "The bylaw's s.140 provision allows 3 units on lots 280 m² or less and 4 units on lots greater than 280 m² in the stated conditions. How SSMUH applies on ALR prairie parcels is a per-parcel question and is not stated."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Floodplain and slope: low land at the south/southeast edge toward Sumas Prairie is floodplain land by the Sumas River; City flood notices name properties south of the Sumas River and along Whatcom Road in evacuation order and alert areas, and the OCP includes a Drainage and Flood Control map (Map 12) — check the parcel in the City's WebMap. The OCP applies a Steep Slope Development Permit Area to slope land, and the McKee plan's open-space network includes geotechnical areas and slopes over 40%."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Slope / heritage diligence: the OCP includes a Steep Slope Development Permit Area for slope land — on McKee-area lots expect geotechnical conditions (the plan's open space network includes geotechnical areas and slopes over 40%) — and in Clayburn Village, confirm the Heritage Conservation Area rules before planning exterior changes."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Steep-slope control: The City OCP includes a Steep Slope Development Permit Area, and the McKee plan protects unstable geotechnical areas and slopes over 40% as open space — the practical reason future multi-family form here is planned as ground-oriented product at the foot, not apartment buildings on the slopes."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Flood / watercourse diligence: Willband Creek runs through the Historic Downtown / central area and is named in City Historic Downtown plan material. The central upland is not Sumas Prairie — but parcel-level floodplain status still requires a City floodplain map check for any lot near the creek, and no lot should be priced as flood-free on the neighbourhood name alone."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "flood"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Strata, co-op and age rules in Abbotsford",
   "id": "strata",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "For a strata townhouse elsewhere in Abbotsford — Form B, depreciation reports, CRF: the strata must provide a Form B Information Certificate (Strata Property Act s. 59); since July 1, 2024 every strata with five or more lots must hold a depreciation report on a five-year cycle (a sub-5-lot strata sits UNDER that threshold), and since November 1, 2023 stratas must contribute at least 10% of the operating fund to the CRF annually."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "strata"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Zoning, suites and small-scale housing in Abbotsford",
   "id": "zoning",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "City frame — Abbotsford: Zoning Bylaw No. 2400-2014 governs: urban RS zones (RS3 540 m² / RS5 420 m² minimum interior lots in City reports), rural A1 at an 8.0 ha minimum subdivision lot area. Suites: Zoning Bylaw s.140.3.8 permits one secondary suite in all single detached dwellings (City-report criteria include a minimum 400 m² lot and 12.0 m frontage); Abbotsford's suite cap is UNDER 50% of the principal dwelling, and a coach/garden suite is capped at 15% of lot area to a maximum of 100 m² — Abbotsford's rules only. SSMUH does not apply to pad tenancies."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "OCP: Abbotsforward 2050 is CURRENT (adopted Dec 2025): The McKee Neighbourhood Plan (Bylaw 3400-2023) sits under it as Schedule E for the mountainside, and Clayburn Village is protected through a Heritage Conservation Area in the OCP."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "City plan: the current Official Community Plan is Abbotsforward 2050, adopted December 2025 (see the currency note at the top) — the 2016 OCP is not current."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Airport Zoning Regulations: where a lot is inside the AZR area on City drawing M-500, building height may not exceed the approach, outer or transitional surfaces, and NAV CANADA and Transport Canada approvals are required before a City building permit — check before planning a tall shop or barn."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "zoning"
  },
  {
   "t": "h",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Other things to know about buying in Abbotsford",
   "id": "other",
   "gen": "cityrules"
  },
  {
   "t": "list",
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Farm-class thresholds (full three-tier form): $2,500 of farm income for parcels of 0.8–4 ha; $2,500 plus 5% of the value of land over 4 ha for larger parcels; $10,000 for parcels under 0.8 ha; applications by October 31."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "other"
  },
  {
   "items": [
    [
     {
      "t": "Rental bylaws void: since November 24, 2022 (long-term); short-term rentals may still be restricted. Directory lines such as \"rentals allowed w/restrictions\" for Junction Creek do not restore a long-term rental ban."
     }
    ]
   ],
   "t": "list",
   "gen": "cityrules-item",
   "topic": "other"
  },
